course: dictionary
lesson: 6801
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one. The word organic is a good example. «Organic farming follows rules such as using fewer artificial chemicals.» The clues are farming and chemicals, so organic means grown without man-made chemicals.
explain: Now read «organic waste goes in the compost bin.» Waste is rubbish, and compost is made from things that rot, so organic here means coming from living things, like leaves and peel. Same word, different meaning. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence.
explain: Some words also tell you how something changes. Organic growth happens naturally and slowly, not forced. When you meet a new meaning, check the clues around it, and use a dictionary if the context is still unclear.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Organic» can mean grown without man-made chemicals, as in «organic food».
fact: In science, «organic» means coming from living things, such as plants and animals.
fact: In chemistry, «organic» substances contain carbon, like sugar, oil and plastics.
fact: «Organic» growth or change happens naturally and slowly, not forced.
exercise: «Organic farming follows rules such as using fewer artificial chemicals.» What does organic mean here? | Very fast and forced | Grown in a hot greenhouse | *Grown without man-made chemicals | Made of plastic
exercise: «Put the organic waste in the compost bin.» What does organic suggest? | Made in a factory | Very shiny and new | Full of man-made chemicals | *Coming from living things, such as leaves and peel
exercise: «The town grew in an organic way, over many years.» What does organic growth mean? | Sudden growth from a building plan | Growth that stops at once | *Natural and slow, not forced | Growth made of plastic
test: «Organic substances contain carbon, like sugar, oil and plastics.» In chemistry, what do organic substances contain? | *Carbon | Salmonella | Only chemicals from a factory | Feathers
test: The code on a British egg starts with 0 organic, 1 free range, 2 barn or 3 cage. What does the first number tell you? | *How the hen lived | The size of the egg | The date the egg was laid | The farm's postcode
exam: Which sentence uses «organic» to mean coming from living things? | The organic growth of the city was forced by a sudden law | The organic light glowed brightly | *Dead leaves and roots, which are organic matter, rot into the soil | Organic food is grown without artificial pesticides
